    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Educated at the Ateneo, Santo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loma-people">Tomas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, Rizal became the best-known writer of the Filipino Propaganda Movement, which sought reforms for the colony under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li><li>His novels Noli Me Tángere and El filibusterismo, critical of the friars and colonial government, angered the Spanish authorities and are taken together as a national epic.</li><li>Though he condemned the Katipunan's uprising, he was tried for rebellion and shot in 1896, and his standing as national hero and his attitude to the revolution remain debated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Rizal's farewell poem, now called Mi último adiós, was hidden in an alcohol stove handed to his family after his execution; he had told his sisters in English, &quot;There is something inside it&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

